sections.body:
Certified data may **not** be required when (FAR 15.403-2):
- prices are based on adequate price competition;
- prices are set by law or regulation;
- a commercial product or service is being bought;
- the head of the contracting activity grants a waiver;
- a commercial contract is being modified (in some cases).
At or below the SAT, certified data are never required. When certified data are not required, you may still ask for other data you need to judge price.

A cost is allowable only if it is (FAR 31.201-2):
- **reasonable** (what a prudent business would pay);
- **allocable** (it benefits this contract);
- consistent with Cost Accounting Standards, if they apply, or generally accepted accounting principles;
- consistent with the contract terms; and
- not limited or disallowed by the cost principles.
FAR 31.205 lists specific costs; for example, entertainment, alcoholic beverages, bad debts and most lobbying costs are unallowable.

Only an **interested party**, an actual or prospective offeror whose direct economic interest would be affected, can protest. It can file with:
- **the agency** (FAR 33.104);
- **GAO** (FAR 33.105, under GAO's own rules in 4 CFR Part 21);
- **the U.S. Court of Federal Claims** (FAR 33.106).
